.TH MOUNT.DYNBLK 8 "2026-09-17" "Dynblk 1.0.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mount.dynblk \- attach and mount existing dynblk storage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount -t dynblk
.I SOURCE TARGET
.RB [ -o " OPTIONS" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The helper is an entry point of the native dynblk executable. It attaches an
existing format-1 container, retains the whole-disk descriptor while mounting,
and arms cookie-protected automatic detach after its last block-layer user
closes. It does not create or format containers and has no resident daemon.
A regular umount releases the mount; open descriptors, mounted partitions,
bind mounts and references retained by lazy unmount delay device teardown.
Manual dynblk load retains its explicit-unload lifetime.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B inner-fstype=TYPE
Mount one concrete inner filesystem type, or auto-detect when omitted.
Negative type filters beginning with no are not accepted.
.TP
.B partition=N
Select a positive partition number. Without this option mount the whole disk;
no partition is selected implicitly.
.TP
.B module=PATH
Load a validated root-owned dynblk.ko when the module is not already loaded.
.TP
.B ro
Attach all backing files read-only and expose a read-only block device.
Selected live state is validated, but roots are not repaired and unused parts
are not removed. Inner filesystem recovery is never forced by this helper.
.TP
.B -f
Validate helper arguments without opening storage or loading modules.
.PP
Ordinary inner filesystem options are forwarded. Negative filesystem type
filters, user mounts, remount, bind and loop setup are not helper operations.
Both native format 1 and split sparse VMDK are supported. Use
format=auto|dynblk|vmdk and cache=writeback|writethrough|none|directsync|unsafe.
The default cache mode is writeback; direct modes currently require ext2/ext4
backing storage. Unsafe deliberately omits durability barriers.
.SH SAFETY
Source paths follow dynblk ownership and namespace rules. The target must
already exist. Execution requires real and effective root, not setuid elevation.
Failed mounts release the helper-owned device; other attachments are not removed.
Asynchronous cleanup can briefly retain file locks after unmount, so helper
loads retry only a transient lock conflict for a bounded interval.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dynblk (8),
.BR mount (8),
.BR umount (8)
